addclaim(96,922,6,[93],'407–423','Paraphrase: the limits-defined envelope of fields is the category of pseudofields, characterized by a universal partial-inverse map.','The commutative von Neumann regular rings, including the zero ring, are exactly rings with a unique quasi-inverse a* satisfying a²a*=a and a(a*)²=a*. Equivalently they invert the represented map Z[t]→Z[t,t^-1]×Z. The displayed product is Z[x,y]/((xy−1)x,(xy−1)y), with t sent to x.','algebra identification and converse stated as known','Put e=xy in the quotient. The relations give e²=e, x=ex, y=ey. On the e-component xy=1; on the 1−e-component x=y=0. Chinese remainder decomposition gives Z[t,t^-1]×Z. Uniqueness follows by the standard commuting generalized-inverse calculation, so adjoining the inverse is a property, not additional nonunique structure. Olivier’s reflection T(A) identifies the universal category and inverts exactly the spectrum/residue-field test used here.','Olivier1967–68 Proposition5 printed6-04 constructs the universal absolutely flat ring with constructible spectrum and unchanged residue fields. Stevenson2014 Proposition3.3/Theorem3.9 and Caramello–Johnstone2009§1 give the explicit quasi-inverse presentation. The orthogonality implication is checked through that reflection, rather than inferred merely from a shared name.',['Olivier1967','Stevenson2014','CaramelloJohnstone2009'])
addclaim(97,922,6,[93,94,95],'422–435;453–462','Paraphrase: units may be constructed by finite limits, and a field is given by an isomorphism from zero plus units.','In a finitely complete category, the unit object A× of a commutative ring object is the subobject of A defined by pairs (x,y) with xy=1. In a Grothendieck topos a geometric field is characterized by 1⊔A×→A, zero on the first summand, being an isomorphism.','units question answered at426; the 1⊔ term is deleted in two earlier occurrences and restored later','Uniqueness of y makes the projection from inverse pairs monic, and multiplication and inversion restrict by the ring equations. In a topos the coproduct map is monic exactly when zero and units are disjoint (0≠1), and epic exactly when every element is locally zero or invertible. Both conditions together are equivalent to an isomorphism. Omitting the zero summand would incorrectly require every element, including zero, to be invertible.','Caramello–Johnstone2009§1 writes the exact coherent field axiom and nontriviality condition. The finite-limit inverse-pair construction and coproduct equivalence are independently re-proved here.',['CaramelloJohnstone2009'])
addclaim(98,922,6,[94,95],'428–473','Paraphrase: first the field classifier is proposed as a presheaf topos; that paragraph is deleted, and the next sheet replaces it by sheaves.','The coherent theory of fields is classified by sheaves on the opposite of finitely presented commutative regular rings, with the topology generated by the disjoint zero/invertible decompositions and the empty cover of the zero ring. Its ordinary coherent ring-language formulation is not of presheaf type.','corrected by himself later, file07 scan96 lines1–9','First impose unique quasi-inverses to obtain the presheaf classifier of regular rings. The additional field axioms force the two component quotients R/(a) and R/(aa*−1) to cover and force the zero ring to have an empty cover. These are jointly the decomposition topology. The algebra coproduct in the affine site is not already the coproduct of representable presheaves, which explains the failed shortcut.','Caramello–Johnstone2009§1pp1–3 gives exactly this site and topology; Beke2004 Corollary2.2 proves no coherent presheaf-type axiomatization of fields exists. The category of fields nevertheless is finitely accessible, so a different infinitary enriched presentation can be of presheaf type; this does not restore the deleted assertion.',['CaramelloJohnstone2009','BekePresheafType'])
claims[-1]['lines'].append(source_file(7)+':1–9');claims[-1]['scans'] += [96]
addclaim(99,922,6,[95],'471–473','Paraphrase: finite limits, sums and balancedness should suffice for the same field-classification correspondence.','The stated Grothendieck-topos result is valid; the extension to every finitely complete, finitely cocomplete balanced category is not justified by those axioms alone. Stable disjoint coproducts and the relevant effective descent/exactness conditions must be specified before one can assert the same classification.','general statement followed by the finite-sums question and a doubtful marginal word','Balancedness says only that a mono-epi is invertible. It does not imply disjoint, pullback-stable coproducts, nor that a cover relation in a site is interpreted by the intended descent property. Thus the preceding topos argument supplies no proof in the advertised generality. No counterexample to every possible interpretation of the underdefined functor condition is claimed.','The exact field-site theorem in Caramello–Johnstone§1 and the finitary exactness-doctrine results of Di Liberti–Osmond6.4.5–6.4.6 give positive settings. Searches did not identify the broad balanced-category formulation as a precise theorem; the residual task is to specify its exactness and preservation conditions.',['CaramelloJohnstone2009','DiLibertiOsmond2025'],'C',confidence='medium')
addclaim(100,922,7,[96],'13–31','Paraphrase: does the construction of non-zero-divisors commute with inverse images of topoi? The universal ring calculation gives only units.','The internally defined subobject of regular elements of the universal ring in the ring-classifying presheaf topos is its unit subobject. Formation of regular elements in arbitrary rings is therefore not geometric and need not commute with inverse image.','negative argument supplied; final displayed conclusion has a likely logical reversal','A section a over a finitely presented ring A is internally regular only if it stays regular under every finitely presented base change. If a is not a unit, A/(a) is nonzero and finitely presented over A; there a becomes zero and fails regularity. Conversely units stay units and regular. Pull back to the Set model Z: 2 is regular but not a unit, so the regular-element subobject cannot commute with that inverse image. The quotient argument avoids any unjustified finite-presentation assumption on residue fields.','Caramello–Johnstone§1 supplies the ring/field classifier framework; the exact universal-base-change computation is independently re-proved. It answers the manuscript’s question without identifying domain and field theories.',['CaramelloJohnstone2009','SGA4I1972'])

addproblem(55,'The pseudofield envelope and orthogonality test',[occurrence(6,[93],'407–423')],'Are the rings orthogonal to the spectrum/residue-field bijections exactly pseudofields?','Yes via the universal absolute-flat reflection, with the zero ring included and the unique quasi-inverse equations retained.','C096 matches Olivier Proposition5 and the explicit presentations in Stevenson3.3/3.9 and Caramello–Johnstone§1.',['Olivier1967','Stevenson2014','CaramelloJohnstone2009'],worth='Worth a concise reconstruction of the orthogonality proof and the explicit ring decomposition; the existence of the reflection itself is established.')
addproblem(56,'Finite-limit construction of units',[occurrence(6,[93,94],'422–428','question immediately answered')],'Can the unit subobject of a ring object be defined by finite limits?','Yes, as the inverse-pair equalizer projected to the first coordinate.','C097 gives the construction and explains the necessary zero summand in the field axiom.',['CaramelloJohnstone2009'],worth='Commentary work only: the sheet answers the question, and the inverse-pair proof makes its finite-limit nature explicit.')
addproblem(57,'The withdrawn presheaf field classifier',[occurrence(6,[94,95],'428–473','presheaf conclusion canceled'),occurrence(7,[96],'1–9','sheaf-topos correction')],'Does the quotient category alone classify fields, or must a topology be added?','A decomposition topology and the empty cover of the zero ring must be added for the coherent field theory.','C098 matches Caramello–Johnstone§1 and Beke Corollary2.2; it distinguishes a different presheaf-type presentation with the same Set models.',['CaramelloJohnstone2009','BekePresheafType'],worth='A high-value commentary comparison: the manuscript correction anticipates exactly the distinction between quasi-inverse algebra and geometric field covering axioms.')
addproblem(58,'How far the field construction extends beyond topoi',[occurrence(6,[95],'471–473')],'Do finite sums and balancedness suffice in the final general category statement?','One must first specify stable disjoint sums, effective quotient/descent conditions and the preservation class; topoi and suitable exact doctrines are positive settings.','C099 records the exact gap and the searched positive references without inventing a theorem for the ambiguous formulation.',['CaramelloJohnstone2009','DiLibertiOsmond2025'],worth='Worth a bounded task only after choosing a precise exactness doctrine; an unrestricted balanced-category claim is presently too ambiguous to develop.')
addproblem(59,'Are regular elements a geometric construction?',[occurrence(7,[96],'13–31')],'Does the regular-element subobject commute with geometric inverse image?','No; universal regularity gives units, while Z has the nonunit regular element2.','C100 supplies the finite-presentation-safe quotient argument.',['CaramelloJohnstone2009','SGA4I1972'],worth='Commentary work only: the negative answer is complete, with one final formula reserved for an ink check.')

